WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:03.920
Yes, Amazon's "The Rings of Power"&nbsp;
takes place thousands of years before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:07.980
"The Hobbit" and "The Lord of the Rings." But&nbsp;
to immortal beings, what's a few millennia?

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:11.840
"The Rings of Power" actor Robert&nbsp;
Aramayo said of his character Elrond,

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:14.560
"When I read about him, I'm always&nbsp;
really interested in the way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:17.680
Tolkien talks about [how Elrond&nbsp;
is] counted amongst the immortals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:22.080
which is an interesting thing. Counted, it&nbsp;
doesn't necessarily say that he is one."

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:26.640
Biologically speaking, Elrond is definitely&nbsp;
immortal. But he's also related to mortal Men,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:30.720
immortal Elves, and even the angelic&nbsp;
Maiar. Elrond's critical role as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.640
the bridge between many of Middle-earth's&nbsp;
peoples, combined with his eternal nature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:37.120
keeps him in the spotlight for a very long time.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:41.200
"I was there the day the strength of men failed."

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:44.800
In "The Rings of Power," he's a scrappy&nbsp;
young politician. By the time we see him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:48.320
in "The Hobbit" films, he's the leader of&nbsp;
Rivendell and a member of the White Council.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:52.320
He continues in that capacity in "The Lord of&nbsp;
the Rings," helping point characters including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:56.960
Frodo and Aragorn in the right direction. By the&nbsp;
end of that story, he does leave Middle-earth for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:01.900
a quiet retirement in the Blessed Realm in the&nbsp;
West. By that point, he's more than earned it.

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:06.480
Over the years, Tolkien struggled with Galadriel's&nbsp;
role in Middle-earth. He wrote his way through it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:10.160
it seems. The book "Unfinished Tales" has&nbsp;
an entire section titled "The History of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:13.840
Galadriel and Celeborn [KELL-uh-born]." While&nbsp;
full of interesting facts and parts of stories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:17.840
the text contradicts itself and presents&nbsp;
multiple versions of the heroine's role.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:21.280
One thing that's clear is that Tolkien&nbsp;
wanted Galadriel to play an essential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:24.800
role in the Second Age, and the "The Rings&nbsp;
of Power" showrunners have used that as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:29.920
opportunity to make her essentially the series&nbsp;
lead, at least so far. Galadriel was also a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:33.840
founding member of the White Council during&nbsp;
the Third Age. This allowed Peter Jackson to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:37.840
insert her into "The Hobbit" films when that&nbsp;
group takes down the Necromancer in Mirkwood.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:40.320
And the Lady of Lorien plays an&nbsp;
essential role in "The Lord of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:43.760
the Rings," hosting and helping the&nbsp;
Fellowship on their way to Mordor.

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:50.080
"I will diminish, and go into&nbsp;
the West, and remain Galadriel."

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:53.840
In Peter Jackson's adaptation of "The Lord&nbsp;
of the Rings," the Dark Lord Sauron's eye&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:57.520
sits perched atop his massive tower&nbsp;
of Barad-dûr in the Land of Shadow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:00.000
scanning the countryside for any sign of trouble.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.560
Sauron also appears in "The&nbsp;
Hobbit" trilogy of films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:06.640
this time in the form of the Necromancer.&nbsp;
Originally a minor character, Jackson took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:11.280
Sauron's foreboding presence and inserted it as a&nbsp;
major plot point in his own version of that story.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:15.440
Sauron's presence is likewise felt from&nbsp;
the beginning of "The Rings of Power."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:19.280
He's briefly in the prologue for the first&nbsp;
episode. Then his sigil starts showing up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:22.880
everywhere, and he's even named multiple&nbsp;
times. When he does finally reveal himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:26.960
it will likely be in his more attractive&nbsp;
form as Annatar, the Lord of Gifts.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:31.360
There's one more character in the movies and&nbsp;
the show: Gil-galad has a brief cameo at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:35.120
beginning of "The Fellowship of the Ring"&nbsp;
and is already in Prime Video's series.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:37.360
Besides that small role and the three big ones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:41.360
no one else from Peter Jackson's films is&nbsp;
confirmed for "The Rings of Power" as of now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:44.800
But a few other names could join&nbsp;
that list as the story plays out.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:48.400
First off, there's Gandalf. Fans have been&nbsp;
debating who the man in the fire could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:51.840
since he first appeared in the marketing.&nbsp;
While that mystery hasn't been solved in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:55.600
early episodes, it's clear that the show&nbsp;
could be working Gandalf into the story.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:59.600
Legolas is another. Since the adventuring&nbsp;
elf's backstory is relatively vague,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:01.520
there's a chance that we&nbsp;
could see the character pop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:04.880
up in "The Rings of Power." Jackson found a&nbsp;
way to fit him into "The Hobbit," after all.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:08.640
Some other possibilities include Thranduil,&nbsp;
who was the king of Mirkwood long before he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:12.880
appeared in "The Hobbit." There's also Treebeard.&nbsp;
We know that the new series will have Ents, and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:17.840
according to the lore, Treebeard&nbsp;
was their leader at that time.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:22.699
"It takes a long time to&nbsp;
say anything in old Entish."

